<section class="page-hero page-hero--legal">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">Legal</span>
    <h1 class="page-hero__title">Cookie Policy</h1>
    <p class="page-hero__lead">
      This policy explains the cookies and similar browser storage that <%= site.name %> uses,
      why we use them, and how you can control them.
    </p>
    <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: <%= lastUpdated %></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container prose legal">

    <h2>1. What are cookies and local storage?</h2>
    <p>
      Cookies are small text files a website can store in your browser. Similar technologies —
      such as <em>local storage</em> — let a site keep small pieces of information on your device.
      Together they are used to remember preferences and, in some cases, to support advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>2. How we use them</h2>
    <p>
      <%= site.name %> is designed to be light on cookies. We currently use only what is needed to
      run the site and to remember your choices. Non-essential technologies (such as advertising
      cookies) are used only where you have consented.
    </p>

    <h3>Strictly necessary</h3>
    <ul class="check-list check-list--muted">
      <li>
        <strong>Cookie preference.</strong> When you accept or decline non-essential cookies, we
        store that choice in your browser’s local storage so we can respect it on your next visit.
        This is required for the site to honour your decision and cannot be switched off.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Advertising (only with consent, when enabled)</h3>
    <p>
      To help keep <%= site.name %> free, we may show ads using Google AdSense. When advertising is
      enabled and you have consented (where consent is required), Google and its partners may set
      cookies to serve and measure ads.
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list check-list--muted">
      <li>These cookies are set by Google, not by <%= site.name %>.</li>
      <li>They may be used to show ads based on your prior visits to this and other sites.</li>
      <li>You can manage or opt out via <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google Ads Settings</a> and <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">aboutads.info/choices</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Analytics</h3>
    <p>
      We do not currently use analytics cookies. If we add analytics in the future, we will update
      this policy and, where required, ask for your consent first.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Managing your choices</h2>
    <p>
      We currently do not set optional analytics or advertising cookies, so no consent banner is
      shown. If we introduce optional cookies in the future (for example, when advertising is
      enabled), we will show a consent banner with three clear options — <strong>Accept all</strong>,
      <strong>Decline optional</strong>, and <strong>Manage preferences</strong> — where you can turn
      optional categories on or off individually. Strictly necessary storage is always on because the
      site cannot work without it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Optional analytics and advertising technologies are never loaded unless you allow them. When a
      banner is in use, consent defaults to “denied” until you choose, and your choice is shared with
      advertising and analytics tools using Google Consent Mode so they respect your decision — and a
      <strong>Cookie settings</strong> link in the footer lets you change it at any time.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also control cookies through your browser settings — most browsers let you block or
      delete cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>4. More information</h2>
    <p>
      For how cookies fit into our wider handling of data, see our
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>. Questions? Email
      <a href="mailto:<%= site.privacyEmail %>"><%= site.privacyEmail %></a>.
    </p>

  </div>
</section>
